🏆 4. My preferred model: The Creator-First Game Economy

Here’s the system I would explore for Freedom World.

Layer 1 — The game

Creators build and publish the game. Players can purchase or access it under clear terms.

Layer 2 — The game economy

Players can buy optional digital content and participate in supported marketplace activities.

Layer 3 — The game token

If a token genuinely improves the experience, it can provide clearly defined utility or other legally reviewed rights.

Layer 4 — Creator compensation

Creators receive their agreed share of game revenue. If token-related proceeds are intended to benefit them, the agreements specify exactly how.

Layer 5 — Freedom World

Freedom World supplies the platform, creator tools, community infrastructure, and distribution opportunities in return for its agreed compensation and continuing contractual rights.

This model preserves your main objective: creators should receive the majority of the value they generate.

It also gives us flexibility. Some games might benefit from tokens, while others might work better with ordinary purchases. We shouldn’t force every game to have cryptocurrency.

⸻

⚖️ 5. One major decision before issuing game tokens

There is a significant difference between selling a token for gameplay functionality and selling one that people buy primarily because they expect the game’s success to increase its price.

Depending on the token’s actual design, marketing, rights, and distribution, the latter could trigger U.S. securities laws and other financial regulations. Calling something a utility token does not automatically exempt it from those laws.

If we want to explore game tokens that are tradeable or tied to financial returns, we should obtain qualified legal advice before selling them, promising returns, or launching a trading market.

We should also account for smart-contract security, wallet recovery, consumer disclosures, applicable taxes, and the possibility that players lose money.

There’s no need to take those risks just to begin building the game ecosystem.

⸻

🚀 Here’s what I would do first

I would design The Portal Awakens around a conventional game purchase and creator-revenue model, while documenting how a future game token could fit into the architecture.

We would not issue the token or promise it will appreciate yet.

First, we’d answer four questions:

1. What does buying the token actually give the player? Game access, optional features, digital items, contractual revenue rights, or something else?
2. How do creators earn from it? Direct sales, contractual revenue distributions, game purchases, or a combination?
3. Can the token be traded? If so, under what rules and with what legal and security protections?
4. Why does the game need a token at all? What useful function does it provide that ordinary payments cannot provide as simply?

If we can answer those questions convincingly, we can evaluate whether the token creates real value.

My bottom line

I like the idea of giving each game its own optional economic ecosystem. It could help creators experiment with new business models and give their communities more ways to participate.

But I wouldn’t make the token’s speculative value the foundation of the game or the creators’ earnings. I’d make the game itself valuable, ensure creators are paid fairly, and use a token only where it adds a genuine, well-defined benefit.

That gives us a much stronger starting point for building a sustainable Freedom World creator economy. 🌎🎮